// M4: a bare `spawn` is a child of its `scope { }`, and it must be a DIRECT statement of that scope. The
// handle is declared at the spawn's own depth while the barrier joins at the scope's brace, so a spawn in
// a nested block declared a handle the join could not name — this program emitted invalid C ("use of
// undeclared identifier '__kama_iso0'") in fully safe kama with no `unsafe` anywhere.
//
// Hoisting the declaration would not have saved it: one variable holds one handle while this loop starts
// THREE, so the barrier would join only the last child and orphan the other two. A scope's children are
// the `spawn`s written directly in it. Per-item work is `parallel_for`; a conditional child puts the `if`
// outside the `scope` (see tests/scope_spawn_conditional.kama). This MUST fail to compile.
import { std::concurrent::Isolate };

type resource Job {
    int32 n;
    public ctor make(int32 n) { this.n = n; return this; }
}

fn void run(Job j) { }   // the bundle is consumed here; the body is irrelevant to the rule under test

fn int32 main() {
    int32 i = 0;
    scope {
        while (i < 3) {
            Job j = Job.make(n: i);
            spawn run(j: give j);   // ERROR: not a direct statement of the `scope`
            i = i + 1;
        }
    }
    return 0;
}
